{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg6avJqUzRyyMSoG77DBR", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Mostly clear to empirical economists and IS researchers, but a general Management Science reader would hit four gaps:\n\n- **\"This literature\" and \"the effect\"** are never named. The opening never says that the outcome is sales or that the question is how review valence affects it, so the reader can't tell what is being identified.\n- **\"Valence\"** is jargon outside marketing and IS.\n- **\"Exogenous variation\" and \"quasi-random assignment\"** are asserted, but the mechanism is left implicit. A reader can't see why unequal moderation would be unrelated to product quality.\n- **\"Positive and significant\"** is vague, but I can't supply a magnitude without the number.\n\nI kept your claims, voice, and the 3.4 million figure, and added no citations.\n\n```text\nReviews that are more favorable tend to accompany products that are better, which makes it hard to\ntell whether a review's valence (how positive or negative it is) causes sales or merely reflects\nunobserved product quality. This endogeneity of review valence with respect to unobserved product\nquality is a well-known obstacle to identification in the literature on how online reviews affect\nsales. We exploit platform-level heterogeneity in content moderation as a source of exogenous\nvariation, that is, variation in which reviews are seen that is unrelated to product quality.\nSpecifically, we leverage the fact that platforms differentially apply moderation policies across\nreview cohorts, which generates quasi-random assignment conditional on observed covariates. Using a\ndataset of 3.4 million reviews, we estimate the effect of review valence on sales and find it to be\npositive and significant. Our identification strategy improves upon prior approaches that rely on\ninstrumental variables of questionable validity.\n```\n\nThree things to check:\n\n- **Added gloss.** \"Variation in which reviews are seen that is unrelated to product quality\" is my plain-language reading of \"exogenous variation.\" If moderation works through something other than visibility, such as removal or editing, adjust it.\n- **Opening sentence.** I added an intuitive lead sentence and kept your original sentence after it. If you'd rather start with your original sentence, delete the first one and the text still stands.\n- **Effect size.** For this venue, \"positive and significant\" will read as thin without a magnitude, for example an elasticity or percentage change in sales. I left it alone because I can't add a number."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 2763, "cache_read_input_tokens": 649, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 2763}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "19b6103d-cbfc-505f-8dc1-c40cc8379cf6", "uuid": "6beebc37-e78a-4e03-a26b-9a6c39531b44", "timestamp": "2026-10-16T20:57:49.637Z", "request_id": "req_011Cg6avJc6myUCmBPvG8NXJ"}
